WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.9.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.760
Smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.520
By the end, you'll know how a

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.160
smart account actually works under the hood,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.448
and how to use its features, recovery,

00:00:11.448 --> 00:00:14.206
limits, passkeys, without opening up new risks.

00:00:15.506 --> 00:00:16.796
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.796 --> 00:00:19.797
First, what actually changes when a wallet becomes a

00:00:19.797 --> 00:00:23.211
smart account, and the standard that makes it possible.

00:00:23.211 --> 00:00:27.901
Second, the features that standard unlocks, recovery, limits, passkeys.

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:31.062
And third, the new risks those same features introduce,

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:33.151
so you use them without a blind spot.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.952
Every wallet so far in this program has

00:00:36.952 --> 00:00:39.954
had exactly one rule: whoever holds the private

00:00:39.954 --> 00:00:42.380
key can move the funds, full stop.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.503
A smart account changes that single

00:00:44.503 --> 00:00:46.925
rule into whatever rules you choose.

00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:49.356
That's a real upgrade in what's possible,

00:00:49.356 --> 00:00:51.324
and it's also a real expansion of

00:00:51.324 --> 00:00:52.945
what you have to understand.

00:00:54.246 --> 00:00:55.411
Two kinds of wallet.

00:00:56.711 --> 00:00:58.351
So, side by side.

00:00:58.351 --> 00:01:01.947
An EOA, an externally owned account, the wallet type

00:01:01.947 --> 00:01:05.542
you've used all module, is controlled by exactly one

00:01:05.542 --> 00:01:08.963
private key: that key signs, or nothing moves.

00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:11.933
A smart account is a contract wallet instead.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:14.053
Its rules, who can sign, how many

00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:16.797
signatures, what limits apply, are code you

00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:18.543
choose, not a fixed default.

00:01:19.842 --> 00:01:23.194
Here's the standard that makes this work

00:01:23.194 --> 00:01:28.727
without changing Ethereum itself: ERC-4337, account abstraction.

00:01:28.727 --> 00:01:30.906
You sign an intent, called a user

00:01:30.906 --> 00:01:33.962
operation, instead of a normal transaction.

00:01:33.962 --> 00:01:37.466
A bundler, a specialised network participant, collects

00:01:37.466 --> 00:01:40.442
user operations and submits them on-chain.

00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:42.598
A paymaster can step in and pay

00:01:42.598 --> 00:01:44.619
the gas for you, in USDC, for

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:48.152
instance, instead of the network's native token.

00:01:48.152 --> 00:01:51.199
And the entry point contract validates everything

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:54.125
and finally executes your smart account's rules.

00:01:55.426 --> 00:01:56.516
Quick check.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.361
What does a paymaster actually do?

00:02:02.781 --> 00:02:05.269
[[pause 4]] The answer: it pays gas on

00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:07.820
your behalf, which is what lets you pay

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.499
network fees in a token like USDC instead

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:12.221
of the chain's native coin.

00:02:13.521 --> 00:02:14.496
What it unlocks.

00:02:15.796 --> 00:02:18.273
Once your wallet is a contract you control,

00:02:18.273 --> 00:02:21.051
all of this becomes possible in one account.

00:02:21.051 --> 00:02:23.736
Multisig, more than one key required.

00:02:23.736 --> 00:02:27.746
Social recovery, trusted guardians who can restore access.

00:02:27.746 --> 00:02:30.216
Spending limits, small moves need less.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:33.307
Session keys, a temporary key scoped to one app,

00:02:33.307 --> 00:02:36.571
which Lesson fourteen point two covers in full.

00:02:36.571 --> 00:02:38.572
Batching, approve and swap in a

00:02:38.572 --> 00:02:40.561
single step instead of two.

00:02:40.561 --> 00:02:44.033
And passkeys, logging in with your device's fingerprint

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.513
or face instead of typing a seed phrase.

00:02:47.813 --> 00:02:51.328
Walk through social recovery as it actually plays out.

00:02:51.328 --> 00:02:52.856
Normally, you just sign with

00:02:52.856 --> 00:02:54.473
your passkey, day to day.

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:55.993
Say you lose your phone.

00:02:55.993 --> 00:02:58.255
Your guardians, people you chose in

00:02:58.255 --> 00:03:00.943
advance, can jointly request recovery.

00:03:00.943 --> 00:03:03.697
That request sits for a cancellable delay, say

00:03:03.697 --> 00:03:06.458
forty eight hours, before it takes effect.

00:03:06.458 --> 00:03:08.538
If that request is really you, you

00:03:08.538 --> 00:03:10.918
do nothing, and access is restored.

00:03:10.918 --> 00:03:13.175
If it isn't, if someone's trying to steal

00:03:13.175 --> 00:03:15.862
the account through your guardians, you cancel it

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.851
in that window, and the attack fails.

00:03:19.151 --> 00:03:20.241
Quick check.

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:22.616
What is social recovery, in one sentence?

00:03:27.036 --> 00:03:30.322
The answer: trusted guardians can restore access to

00:03:30.322 --> 00:03:33.356
your account, usually after a cancellable delay.

00:03:34.656 --> 00:03:36.694
Two of those six are worth a closer

00:03:36.694 --> 00:03:39.072
look, because they change what you do day

00:03:39.072 --> 00:03:41.806
to day, not just what's possible in theory.

00:03:41.806 --> 00:03:45.373
Batching turns a two-step action, approve a token, then

00:03:45.373 --> 00:03:48.558
swap it, into one signature instead of two, which

00:03:48.558 --> 00:03:52.871
also means one less separate approval sitting around afterward.

00:03:52.871 --> 00:03:55.326
And a passkey is backed by your device's

00:03:55.326 --> 00:03:58.321
own secure hardware: nothing to type, and nothing

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.896
a phishing site can trick you into pasting.

00:04:02.196 --> 00:04:04.547
Session keys deserve one honest caveat

00:04:04.547 --> 00:04:06.656
here, and a full lesson later.

00:04:06.656 --> 00:04:08.760
A session key is a temporary key,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.113
scoped to one specific app, so you're

00:04:11.113 --> 00:04:14.136
not re-approving that app every single time.

00:04:14.136 --> 00:04:16.521
Scoped correctly, it's convenient.

00:04:16.521 --> 00:04:18.580
Scoped too broadly, it's a standing

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:20.711
approval you forgot you granted.

00:04:20.711 --> 00:04:24.981
Lesson 14.2 covers exactly how to scope one safely.

00:04:26.281 --> 00:04:30.310
One more standard worth naming: EIP-7702,

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.351
part of Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade.

00:04:34.351 --> 00:04:37.578
It lets an ordinary EOA wallet temporarily, or

00:04:37.578 --> 00:04:42.246
permanently, delegate to smart-account code, gaining these features

00:04:42.246 --> 00:04:45.086
without ever moving to a new address.

00:04:45.086 --> 00:04:46.301
It's powerful.

00:04:46.301 --> 00:04:49.728
It's also exactly the phishing target Lesson 1.7

00:04:49.728 --> 00:04:53.294
warned you about: a malicious delegation looks, to

00:04:53.294 --> 00:04:56.861
an untrained eye, like any other signature request.

00:04:58.161 --> 00:04:59.096
A worked setup.

00:05:00.396 --> 00:05:04.221
Here's a realistic shape for one operating wallet's limits.

00:05:04.221 --> 00:05:06.779
With just the passkey, day-to-day moves

00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:09.061
are capped around $2,000 a day.

00:05:09.061 --> 00:05:11.916
Above that, it requires both the passkey and

00:05:11.916 --> 00:05:14.897
a hardware key together, 2-of-2, with no daily

00:05:14.897 --> 00:05:17.371
cap, because a bigger move now needs a

00:05:17.371 --> 00:05:19.401
second factor to approve it too.

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:23.582
Put together, one real setup looks like this.

00:05:23.582 --> 00:05:25.687
Day to day: a passkey on your phone,

00:05:25.687 --> 00:05:28.092
capped at two thousand dollars a day.

00:05:28.092 --> 00:05:30.526
Larger moves: that same passkey, plus a

00:05:30.526 --> 00:05:33.442
separate hardware key, two of two required.

00:05:33.442 --> 00:05:36.180
Recovery: three guardians, two of three, with a

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.047
forty eight hour delay you can always cancel.

00:05:39.047 --> 00:05:41.602
Lose your phone, guardians recover you.

00:05:41.602 --> 00:05:44.405
Phone stolen, the thief is capped at two thousand

00:05:44.405 --> 00:05:46.703
a day, and you have forty eight hours to

00:05:46.703 --> 00:05:49.226
cancel any recovery attempt you didn't start.

00:05:50.526 --> 00:05:51.371
New risks.

00:05:52.671 --> 00:05:55.130
Every feature above adds something new to

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.771
verify, not just something new to enjoy.

00:05:57.771 --> 00:06:00.331
The account's contract code itself can have

00:06:00.331 --> 00:06:02.551
bugs, same as any smart contract.

00:06:02.551 --> 00:06:05.170
Module and plugin permissions deserve the

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.591
same scrutiny as a token approval.

00:06:07.591 --> 00:06:10.786
Guardians can collude, if you chose them carelessly.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:13.371
And a smart account's address may simply not

00:06:13.371 --> 00:06:15.266
exist yet on every chain you use.

00:06:16.566 --> 00:06:19.801
Guardian collusion is worth sitting with for a second.

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:22.560
Three guardians who all live in the same house,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.319
or all work for the same company, aren't really

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:28.953
three independent checks, they're one, wearing three hats.

00:06:28.953 --> 00:06:32.371
Choose guardians the same way you'd choose separated

00:06:32.371 --> 00:06:36.305
multisig key holders: people, or institutions, who genuinely

00:06:36.305 --> 00:06:39.078
couldn't coordinate even if they wanted to.

00:06:40.378 --> 00:06:42.163
Last check for this lesson.

00:06:42.163 --> 00:06:45.134
Why does it matter whether your smart account exists

00:06:45.134 --> 00:06:47.768
on the destination chain before you send to it?

00:06:52.188 --> 00:06:55.103
The answer: a smart-account address may not be

00:06:55.103 --> 00:06:57.895
deployed on every chain, so funds sent there

00:06:57.895 --> 00:07:00.252
can be hard, or impossible, to access.

00:07:01.552 --> 00:07:03.937
Your turn, 5 to 15 minutes.

00:07:03.937 --> 00:07:06.292
List which of your wallets are EOAs

00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:09.012
and which are already smart accounts.

00:07:09.012 --> 00:07:11.188
Choose guardians, on paper, so that no

00:07:11.188 --> 00:07:13.497
two of them could plausibly collude.

00:07:13.497 --> 00:07:15.393
And if you're moving to a smart

00:07:15.393 --> 00:07:18.356
account, confirm it's deployed, or deployable, on

00:07:18.356 --> 00:07:20.312
every chain you actually send to.

00:07:21.612 --> 00:07:24.819
To recap: a smart account turns one fixed rule,

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:28.828
one key, into rules you choose, multisig, limits, recovery,

00:07:28.828 --> 00:07:33.137
passkeys, all through a standard called account abstraction.

00:07:33.137 --> 00:07:36.337
EIP-7702 brings those same features

00:07:36.337 --> 00:07:39.304
to an ordinary wallet address.

00:07:39.304 --> 00:07:41.370
And every one of those features is

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.730
also something new to verify, the code,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.209
the permissions, the guardians, the chain.

00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:50.001
That's smart accounts and account abstraction,

00:07:50.001 --> 00:07:51.679
and the end of Module One.

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:55.075
Next up, Module Two: Trading On-Chain, starting with

00:07:55.075 --> 00:07:58.214
how swaps, aggregators and routing actually work.
